{
  "name": "tracked overtime keeps the homeward leg: commitmentTimelines and the return directive survive mid-journey",
  "why": "The proving case for the overtime return leg, hand-built rather than promoted from a real snapshot. A shift scheduled 15:00-17:00 with a 20-minute transition and a 25-minute stored leg home is tracked to 17:15. `after-work-steps-follow-overtime-finish.json` never asserts `commitmentTimelines` or the return leg's own timing, only the after-phase routine steps; this fixture is the one that does. At 17:40 — mid the journey home — the return directive must still be 'Drive to Home' and the timeline's `homeAt`−`freeAt` minutes must still show the leg outstanding. Before `commitment-timeline.md`, the return leg's own arithmetic read the bare scheduled `endsAt` (17:00) instead of the effective end (17:15), so the leg's departure window had already closed by 17:15 and the journey home silently vanished from both the directive and the timeline the moment overtime began. A changed line here means the timeline's overtime edges (`effectiveEndsAt`, `freeAt`, `homeAt`) or the leg-matching they feed moved. Re-baselined for the journey store (the travel time model design note): the stored 25-minute fallback now carries padding — `max(3, 0.15 × 25)`, rounded up — to 29 minutes, since nothing about it is sampled; the mode (resolved from the single available `car`) and the typical/sample-count figures are new, always-emitted fields. Re-baselined for `ranking-consistency`'s maximum-session break: a run reaching its maximum session now owes a 15-minute rest before the day plan offers the same ordinary action again, so each `write` block is followed by a `none`-reason gap instead of running back to back; the return-leg behaviour this fixture pins is unaffected.",
